---
import PostLayout from "../../layouts/PostLayout.astro";

const content = `
<p>
  Manual documentation publishing is the single largest source of preventable documentation errors.
  This post walks through building a GitHub Actions workflow that automatically generates HTML from
  reStructuredText using Sphinx and deploys it to an AWS S3 bucket.
</p>

<h2>What the Pipeline Does</h2>

<ol>
  <li>Triggers on pull request merges (and supports manual dispatch)</li>
  <li>Sets up a Python environment and installs Sphinx dependencies</li>
  <li>Builds HTML from reST source using <code>sphinx-build</code></li>
  <li>Archives the build output as a downloadable artifact</li>
  <li>Deploys the HTML to an S3 bucket via AWS CLI</li>
</ol>

<h2>The Workflow</h2>

<pre><code>name: Docs
on:
  pull_request:
    paths-ignore:
      - 'upload/**'
  workflow_dispatch:
permissions:
  contents: write
jobs:
  docs:
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
    env:
      AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID: \${{ secrets.AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID }}
      AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY: \${{ secrets.AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY }}
      AWS_DEFAULT_REGION: 'us-east-1'
    steps:
      - uses: actions/checkout@v3
      - uses: actions/setup-python@v3

      - name: Install dependencies
        run: pip3 install sphinx==5.3.0 sphinx_rtd_theme

      - name: Build docs
        run: sphinx-build -b html source/ _build

      - name: Upload docs as artifact
        uses: actions/upload-artifact@v3.1.2
        with:
          name: docs_artifact
          path: _build

      - name: Deploy to S3
        run: aws s3 sync _build/ s3://your-bucket/path</code></pre>

<h2>Key Design Decisions</h2>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Trigger on pull request</strong>: Documentation updates deploy automatically when PRs merge. No manual step needed</li>
  <li><strong>Manual dispatch</strong>: The <code>workflow_dispatch</code> trigger allows ad-hoc publishing without a code change</li>
  <li><strong>Artifact archiving</strong>: Each build produces a downloadable snapshot, useful for auditing and rollback</li>
  <li><strong>paths-ignore</strong>: Prevents unnecessary builds when unrelated files change</li>
</ul>

<h2>Result</h2>

<p>
  After setting this up, documentation publishing went from a manual build-and-upload process
  to a single pull request merge. Build errors are caught in CI before reaching production.
  The artifact archive provides a history of every published version.
</p>

<p>
  This pattern works for any Sphinx-based documentation project. The same approach applies
  whether you are hosting on S3, GitHub Pages, or any other static hosting provider.
</p>
`;
---

<PostLayout
  title="Generate HTML from reST Using Sphinx and GitHub Actions"
  date="2023"
  summary="How to build a CI/CD pipeline that automatically generates and deploys documentation from reStructuredText to AWS S3."
>
  <Fragment set:html={content} />
</PostLayout>
